\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Effectively handling variable bindings and substitutions remains a central
challenge when mechanizing programming language metatheory. Compared to
pen-and-paper proofs, binders in proof assistants are a notorious source of
uninteresting technicalities and significant boilerplate. Since the beginning
of proof assistants, many representations have been proposed, including locally
nameless binders, nominal syntax, higher-order abstract syntax, and de Bruijn
indices. However, choosing the right representation is difficult. While de
Bruijn syntax provides a promising representation, developments based on it
still include substantial proof overhead by requiring numerous lemmas to manage
substitution composition and interactions with binders.

On paper, substitution is often an operation that replaces just a single
variable. However, in proof assistants we commonly use parallel substitutions,
which map variables to terms and replace multiple variables simultaneously when
a term is instantiated with them.

\subsection{The Sigma Calculus in \textsc{Autosubst}}

\RWnote{the original sentence called $λσ$ ``terminating and confluent'',
      citing a source about a different system. $λσ$ is confluent on \emph{ground}
      terms but not on open ones, and that failure is the reason the later variants
      exist, which is worth one sentence here, since the whole paper is about which
      variant can be oriented.}

\RW{The $σ$-calculus~\cite{10.1145/96709.96712} treats parallel substitutions as
first-class objects, equipped with primitive operations and a rewrite system
that normalizes substitution expressions. On ground terms that system is
terminating and confluent; on open terms, that is terms with metavariables,
confluence fails. This is the setting of any mechanized metatheory, and the
variants below exist to repair it.} \RWnote{restated. The old text said only that $η$ ``cannot be oriented'',
      which does not explain why $σ_{SP}$ is closed to us. The reason is specific,
      and it is one of the load-bearing facts of the paper, so it now appears here
      and is picked up again in \cref{sec:pre-sig} and \cref{sec:mis-sig}.}

\RW{Two later variants matter here, and they differ on exactly the point this
paper turns on. $σ_{SP}$~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising} adds surjective pairing,
that is the two $η$-laws, and is thereby a complete decision procedure: it decides
every equality between substitution expressions~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163}.
$σ_\Uparrow$~\cite{10.1145/226643.226675} instead keeps lifting $\Uparrow$
primitive and omits $η$; it is confluent on open terms, its rule set is tabulated
by Hardin, Maranget and Pagano~\cite{HMP98}, and it is not complete. We build on
$σ_\Uparrow$ throughout.}

\RW{We cannot use $σ_{SP}$, and the reason is not a limitation of our tooling.
$σ_{SP}$ has no numerals: the variable $n$ is the term
$0[\bsym{\mathsf{wk}}]^n$, zero shifted $n$ times. We want native inductive
variables, $\bsym{\mathsf{zero}}$ and $\bsym{\mathsf{suc}}$, because that is what a
scoped syntax in a proof assistant is built from. Having them forces
the rule $x\,[\,\bsym{\mathsf{wk}}\,] = \bsym{\mathsf{suc}}\,x$, which
identifies the two representations of a variable; and that identification is
precisely what destroys the left-hand side of $η$, which mentions its
substitution twice and so is not left-linear. native inductive variables
and surjective pairing cannot coexist in one rewrite system. $σ_{SP}$ gives up
the variables, the first-class-lifting variant gives up $η$, and we follow the
latter.}

\RW{This is not an argument we ask to be taken on trust. Adding the two
$η$-laws to our rule set was predicted to break confluence from exactly this
reasoning, and then refuted mechanically: the extended system is
non-confluent, reported by three independent tools (\cref{sec:ext}).}

The de~Bruijn algebra, where parallel substitutions are modeled as functions,
constitutes a semantic model of the $σ$-calculus. With a semantic model for the
substitution operations, the $σ$-calculus rules become provable, and therefore
qualify for equational reasoning in proof
assistants~\cite{schafer2015autosubst}. While previous work has exploited these
equations to implement automated normalization procedures, the resulting
equalities remain propositional. \textsc{Autosubst}~\cite{schafer2015autosubst}
provided the first normalization procedure using the $σ$-calculus rules for
unscoped syntax definitions inside the Rocq proof assistant. To normalize terms
with parallel substitutions one needs to apply a tactic inside a proof goal.

The successor \textsc{Autosubst 2}~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101} is now
regarded as the gold standard for libraries that handle substitution in proof
assistants. \textsc{Autosubst 2} supports intrinsically scoped syntax where the
syntax data type is indexed over the number of free variables and variable
usage is restricted to the variables in scope by construction. Furthermore,
\textsc{Autosubst 2} generates proofs, for the $σ$-calculus rules that follow
by induction over the syntax, from higher-order abstract syntax descriptions.
It also enables reasoning about syntaxes with multiple, mutually dependent,
sorts by packaging parallel substitutions over different sorts into vectors.

In particular, \textsc{Autosubst 2}, in contrast to the first version, includes
first-class support for renamings, a special case of substitutions that map
variables to variables. Independently of first-class support in the equational
theory, renamings are required in proof assistants to model substitutions in a
structurally recursive manner that the termination checker
accepts~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1}. The creators of \textsc{Autosubst 2}
extended the equational theory of the $σ$-calculus with additional rules that
handle renamings. The resulting system is not complete anymore, but the authors
conjecture that the extended system preserves
confluence~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising}.

\subsection{Promoting Propositional to Definitional Equalities with Rewriting}

Recent support for user-defined rewrite rules in mainstream proof assistants,
such as Agda~\cite{cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2, 10.1145/3434341} and
Rocq~\cite{leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26}, enables the promotion of
propositional equalities to definitional ones. To retain logical consistency,
the set of equational rules must be confluent and supported by
proofs~\cite{10.1145/3434341}. Agda and other proof assistants perform
higher-order pattern unification~\cite{10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_5} to detect
if a registered rewrite rule can be applied to a stuck term (one that does not
reduce further, given the built-in reduction rules). To do so, Agda requires
that the left-hand side is a stuck term on a defined symbol.

To model parallel substitution in proof assistants, we have essentially two
choices: data types or functions. When we model substitution as functions, some
primitive operations of the $σ$-calculus generate stuck lambdas, thereby
violating the syntactic restriction that the left-hand side must be a stuck
symbol and not a lambda. \RWnote{corrected. The previous version's objection, that constructors make $η$
      unprovable, is no objection at all, since we decline to register $η$ anyway. The real cost is a different law, and it is the same law that
      \cref{sec:introduction} just showed decides the $σ_{SP}$ question.}

\RW{The obvious alternative is to make the primitives syntax: if
extension, or weakening, is a constructor then every left-hand side is stuck by
construction and no opacity machinery is needed. Wadler takes this route,
implementing weakening as a term former and thereby avoiding renamings
altogether~\cite{Wadler_2024}.}

\RW{What it costs is exactly the rule of the previous section. A constructor has
nothing to compute, so $x\,[\,\bsym{\mathsf{wk}}\,] = \bsym{\mathsf{suc}}\,x$ is
no longer derivable: one variable now has two representations,
$\bsym{\mathsf{suc}}\,x$ and $\mathsf{weaken}\,x$, that are not definitionally
equal, and every law that pushes weakening through a term stays propositional.
The same coin is spent three different ways. $σ_{SP}$ gives up native variables
to keep $η$, the syntactic encoding gives up
$x\,[\,\bsym{\mathsf{wk}}\,] = \bsym{\mathsf{suc}}\,x$ to get stuckness for
free, and we give up $η$ to keep both. Functions are what let us make that third
choice: modelled as functions, every law of the system we build is provable
(\cref{sec:mis-sig}), and the
only difficulty left is that nothing is stuck, which is what the rest of this
paper resolves.}

If we wish to rewrite all $σ$-calculus rules, we require more control over the
reduction behavior of symbols. Fortunately, Agda supports opaque
definitions~\cite{gratzer2022controlling}, that allow us to selectively block
and later unfold the implementation of function definitions. With opaque
definitions we can have both: we treat function definitions as stuck symbols,
such that we can apply rewrite rules, but also unfold their definitions to
prove the rules using their computational behavior.

\subsection{Content of this Paper}

\subsection*{Contributions}
\RWnote{the contribution list predated the two-world system, the
      external proofs, the generator and the POPLmark development. Restated as
      four claims, each with the evidence that backs it.}

\RW{We contribute four things.}
\begin{itemize}
      \item \RW{\textbf{A rewrite system.} 72 rules in Agda that make the
            $σ$-calculus with first-class renamings definitional in a
            multi-sorted, intrinsically scoped setting, proved before being
            registered so that logical consistency is preserved. Fifty of them are
            $σ_\Uparrow$ rules, their exact two-world duplicates, or per-constructor
            instances of $σ_\Uparrow$'s traversal rules, which is why the count
            exceeds twice $σ_\Uparrow$'s $23$. The rest are
            forced by renamings and by the mode split. \Cref{fig:decisions}
            records how that set was arrived at, including the alternatives we
            built and abandoned.}
      \item \RW{\textbf{Machine-checked meta-theory of the rule set.} Agda certifies
            local confluence (0 non-joinable critical pairs); externally, AProVE
            proves termination (23.9\,s) and confluence (24.9\,s) of
            the first-order export. Both hold at two signature sizes, $3$ and $8$
            constructors, which is evidence that what is proved is a property of
            the schema rather than of System~F, though it is evidence and not a schematic
            proof.}
      \item \RW{\textbf{A generator.} A single-file tool that emits the whole
            infrastructure from a higher-order abstract syntax description, so the
            construction is not tied to one language.}
      \item \RW{\textbf{An evaluation at scale.} POPLmark Parts 1A, 1B, 2A, 2B and~3
            and POPLmark Reloaded, ${\approx}8700$ lines, on the generated cores, with
            one substitution law that our orientation cannot register, which we
            report as a result rather than hide.}
\end{itemize}

% CHANGED(trs): "confluent" -> "locally confluent".  Agda's
% --local-confluence-check certifies joinability of critical pairs only, and
% termination is not verified (cf. the discussion in the Rewriting section), so
% the unqualified claim was too strong.
As a side effect we support the claim that the extension of the $σ$-calculus
rules with first-class renamings is locally confluent, via Agda's built-in
confluence checker. \RW{Externally, we show that it is confluent and
terminating, proved by AProVE on the first-order export (\cref{sec:ext}).
Agda's local check is one ingredient of that, not the whole claim.} We also show that this approach translates to any
scoped syntax by implementing a code generation script for higher-order
abstract syntax descriptions.

\subsection*{Structure}

\Cref{sec:pre} introduces the required background in Agda and for the
$σ$-calculus. \Cref{sec:mis} demonstrates how to register the $σ$-calculus rules
exemplified by an instantiation for System~F. \Cref{sec:fwk} reports the generator, the POPLmark evaluation and the
external proofs, and closes with future work;
related work in \cref{sec:rwk}
and conclude the paper in \cref{sec:con}.

\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:pre}
We begin with a review of material from the literature. We summarize
\begin{itemize}
      \item Agda and language features relevant to our work: rewrite rules and opaque
            blocks;
      \item multi-sorted and scoped syntax in Agda and
      \item the $σ$-calculus modeled via the de~Bruijn algebra.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{All Agda code presented in this paper can be found in the supplement.}

\subsection{Agda and Relevant Language Features}\label{sec:pre-agd}
Agda is a dependently typed functional language and proof assistant based on
Martin-Löf type theory~\cite{MARTINLOF197573}, where proofs are verified by
type checking. Proofs are significantly easier when terms are
\textit{definitionally equal} (i.e.,\ reduce to the same term). When terms are
not definitionally equal, users must resort to manual equational reasoning
using propositional equality, which is a cumbersome process as Agda often lacks
automated proof-discharge tactics. Maximizing definitional equalities thus
directly reduces manual effort.

Beyond standard features such as indexed inductive types and dependent pattern
matching~\cite{10.1145/3236770}, this work is based on two more advanced
language features.

\subsection*{Rewrite Rules}
User-defined rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3434341, cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2}
extend Agda’s normalization-by-evaluation approach for definitional equalities
by treating propositional equalities as reduction rules.

A function is eligible for rewriting if it is of the following form:
\[
      \AgdaFunction{eq} : (x_1 : A_1) \ldots \to \AgdaFunction{f}\, p_1 \ldots p_n
      \, \AgdaFunction{≡} \, v
\]
In addition it needs to satisfy the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
      \item The head \AgdaFunction{f} is a postulate, defined function, or constructor with
            distinct variable type parameters.
      \item Every variable $x_i$ appears in a pattern position among $p_1, \ldots,
                  p_n$\footnote{The Agda documentation includes a definition for pattern
                  positions:
                  \url{https://Agda.readthedocs.io/en/latest/language/rewriting.html}}.
      \item The left-hand side is stuck.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent For example, the right identity of addition satisfies these criteria:
\ERewrite{} Once the rule is registered via \ERewriteIt{}, Agda treats \AgdaBound{n}
\AgdaFunction{+} \AgdaNumber{0} as definitionally equal to \AgdaBound{n}: \ERewriteEx{}

In addition to the syntactic restrictions, users must ensure that the rewrite
rule set as a whole is terminating and confluent to retain logical consistency.

\RWnote{``we asume [sic] the rewrite system is terminating'' is unnecessary,
      and weaker than what we have.}

\RW{Agda never checks termination under \texttt{--rewriting}: a non-terminating
rule set simply makes the type-checker loop. It does check confluence, via
\texttt{--confluence-check} and \texttt{--local-confluence-check}. Given that the
registered rules are supported by proofs, local confluence together with
termination suffices to preserve consistency~\cite{10.1145/3434341}, by Newman's
lemma. We therefore establish termination externally rather than assume it
(\cref{sec:ext}); AProVE's own confluence proof takes exactly this route, one
\textsf{WCR} step followed by \textsf{Newman}.}

\subsection*{Opaque Blocks}
Opaque definitions~\cite{gratzer2022controlling} let the user treat code as
non-unfolding symbols. This feature is typically used to improve type checking
times, because normally we want all definitions to unfold, to produce as many
definitional equalities as possible. Their use case in this work will be to
declare relevant definitions opaque, such that the stuck symbols qualify for
rewriting.

Definitions within an \AgdaKeyword{opaque} block are visible but treated
abstractly during type checking: \EOpaque{} Outside this block,
\AgdaFunction{forty-two} does not reduce to \AgdaNumber{42}: \EOpaqueExO{}
However, the \AgdaKeyword{unfolding} keyword can be used to expose the
computational behavior of a symbol when needed: \EOpaqueExT{}

\subsection{Multi-Sorted and Scoped Syntax}\label{sec:pre-syn}
\begin{figure*}[t]
      \begin{minipage}[t]{0.44\linewidth}
            \raggedright{}
            \EScoped{}
      \end{minipage}
      \hfill
      \begin{minipage}[t]{0.52\linewidth}
            \raggedright{}
            \EMultiSorted{}
            \EMultiSortedTm{}
      \end{minipage}
      \caption{Classically Scoped vs. Multi-Sorted Scoped Syntax}\label{fig:pre-svm}
\end{figure*}

Many programming languages feature multiple syntactic categories. For instance
System~F~\cite{girard1972, 10.1007/3-540-06859-7_148} has three syntactic
sorts: expressions, types, and kinds~\footnote{Technically kinding for System~F
      is trivial and could be omitted.}. We use intrinsically scoped syntax, to
prevent the construction of ill-scoped terms from our syntax.
\Cref{fig:pre-svm} (left) shows a standard implementation: types are indexed by
the amount of type variables, and expressions by both the amount of type and
expression variables. Variables use de~Bruijn indices of type
\AgdaDatatype{Fin} \AgdaBound{n}, that is, natural numbers strictly less than
the number of free variables.

We see scoped syntax as the optimal balance between fully extrinsic syntax and
intrinsically typed syntax, in which case the expression syntax is indexed
directly over contexts and types. It is powerful enough to detect scoping
related errors, but one does not need to commit to full blown intrinsic typing,
which can be difficult to work with for more complex systems.

Alas, the standard representation requires us to define many substitution
operations across the different sorts (e.g., type-in-type, type-in-expr,
expr-in-expr). This representation is chosen by \textsc{Autosubst 2}, and
explains why they need vector substitutions, that bundle substitutions over the
different sorts alongside additional normalization rules.

To avoid the combinatorial explosion of substitutions over different syntactic
sorts, we unify the syntax in two orthogonal dimensions, presented also in
\cref{fig:pre-svm} (right). Variables and terms form a single datatype
\AgdaBound{S} \AgdaDatatype{⊢[} \AgdaBound{m} \AgdaDatatype{]} \AgdaBound{s}
indexed by a \AgdaDatatype{Mode} \AgdaBound{m}, a target \AgdaDatatype{Sort}
\AgdaBound{s} and a list of sorts \AgdaDatatype{Scope} \AgdaBound{S}. Modes
distinguish constructors for variables and terms. Variables are represented
across multiple sorts simultaneously by natural numbers of type \AgdaBound{S}
\AgdaDatatype{∋} \AgdaBound{s} and act as de~Bruijn indices. The length of
\AgdaBound{S} encodes the amount of variables in scope and the position of
\AgdaBound{s} in \AgdaBound{S} directly corresponds to the de~Bruijn index of
the variable. Using the syntax unified over sorts, we can later define
renamings and substitutions that operate uniformly across all sorts, thereby
avoiding vector substitutions. Unifying over modes, allows us to state some
$σ$-calculus laws parametric in the mode.

\RWnote{added. \AgdaDatatype{Mode} carries the whole push/fold argument of
      \cref{sec:mis-sig}, but was introduced in a single clause.}

\RW{The mode index is not only a device for sharing definitions. A variable and
a term behave differently under a map: renaming a variable yields a variable,
which the applied rules can match again, whereas substituting one yields a
term, which they cannot. Several rules therefore exist in two versions that
point in opposite directions, and \cref{sec:mis-sig} shows where this is
forced.}

\subsection{A Short History of the Sigma Calculus}\label{sec:pre-sig}

\RWnote{\S2.3 used to present a 12-rule table and call it ``the
      $σ$-calculus''. It was neither complete (12 of $σ_\Uparrow$'s 23 rules, with
      every applied rule missing, so nothing in it computes) nor the system we
      build on. A full rule set does not belong in the preliminaries; what the
      reader needs is which calculi exist, which are confluent, and why $η$ is the
      dividing line. The rules themselves are now in \cref{sec:mis-sig}, and
      \cref{fig:decisions} says how ours was arrived at.}


\RW{Explicit-substitution calculi make substitutions into syntax, so that
$t\,[\,σ\,]$ is a term and $σ$ can be composed, extended and pushed under
binders by rewriting. Three of them matter here.}

\RW{\textbf{$σ$}~\cite{10.1145/96709.96712} is the original. Lifting is
\emph{defined}, $\Uparrow σ := \bsym{\mathsf{zero}} \bsym{\cdot} (σ
\bsym{⨟} \bsym{\mathsf{wk}})$, and the two $η$-laws, surjective pairing,
are part of the theory. Both versions of \textsc{Autosubst} build on it.}

\RW{\textbf{$σ_\Uparrow$}~\cite{10.1145/226643.226675, HMP98} keeps $\Uparrow$
primitive and drops $η$. It recovers confluence on open terms, those with
metavariables, which is what $λσ$ loses and what a mechanized development
needs. Its 23 rules, tabulated in figures 1 and~2 of~\cite{HMP98}, split into the
applied rules (a variable meets a map), the
traversal rules (one per constructor), the map algebra, and a set of
$\fatsemi$-companions that exist only because associativity is oriented. This
is the calculus we build on.}

\RW{\textbf{$σ_{SP}$}~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising} adds the $η$-laws back and
is thereby complete: it decides every equality between substitution
expressions~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163}.}

\RW{Primitive lifting and the absence of $η$ stand or fall together. Eliminate
lifting and $\mathsf{lift\text{-}id}$ ($\Uparrow\bsym{\mathsf{id}} =
\bsym{\mathsf{id}}$) stops holding by computation, because $\Uparrow$ then
unfolds to $\bsym{\mathsf{zero}} \bsym{\cdot} (\bsym{\mathsf{id}} \bsym{⨟}
\bsym{\mathsf{wk}})$, and only $η\mathsf{\text{-}id}$ reconciles the two. And
$η$ is unavailable to us, as \cref{sec:introduction} said:
$σ_{SP}$ has no numerals, so it can afford $η$; we have native
$\bsym{\mathsf{zero}}$ and $\bsym{\mathsf{suc}}$, so we cannot. Concretely, the
$η$-law's left-hand side $(\bsym{\mathsf{zero}}[σ]) \bsym{\cdot}
(\bsym{\mathsf{wk}} \bsym{⨟} σ)$ mentions $σ$ twice and is therefore not
left-linear: any rule that rewrites one occurrence destroys the pattern for the
other, and $x\,[\,\bsym{\mathsf{wk}}\,] = \bsym{\mathsf{suc}}\,x$ is exactly
such a rule. Completeness and orientability are incompatible here, and a paper
about rewriting must give up completeness.}

\RW{The price is four laws, the two $η$-laws and their renaming-world twins,
which we prove but do not register. \Cref{sec:eval} reports what that costs
in practice, and the answer is nothing we could detect: across the whole
POPLmark development none of them is ever applied by hand.}

\RW{One further ingredient is not in any of these calculi. Defining
instantiation for a substitution requires pushing under binders, which requires
weakening the terms in the substitution, which is instantiation again, a
loop no termination checker accepts. The standard repair is to define renamings
first~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1}; \textsc{Autosubst 2} takes the further step of
making renamings \emph{first-class} and extending the equational theory with
rules that relate the two~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101}. The extended system is
no longer complete, and its confluence was left as a
conjecture~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising}. We do not settle that conjecture, since
the rule set we register is not theirs, but we do answer the corresponding
question for a concrete two-world system, with the machine-checked proofs of
\cref{sec:ext}.}

\input{decisions}

\RW{\Cref{fig:decisions} previews where this lands, and is about the
process rather than the result: six questions, the answer we took, and for each of them the alternative we built
or attempted first and what forced us off it. Only the first was a matter of taste. Which rules each answer then buys
is \cref{sec:mis-sig}'s subject, and is deliberately kept out of the figure.}

\RW{For the semantics we use the de~Bruijn algebra, which models substitutions
as functions and thereby makes the rules provable rather than merely
postulated~\cite{schafer2015autosubst}. Variables are natural numbers, terms
are $t := x \mid \bsym{λ.}\, t \mid t_1\, t_2$, and a substitution maps
variables to terms; reading $σ$ as a list $[t_0, t_1, \dots]$ motivates the
primitives:}
\begin{align*}
      (t \textcolor{agdablue}{\cdot} σ)(\textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{zero}})    & := t    & \textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{id}}(x) & := x                                      \\
      (t \textcolor{agdablue}{\cdot} σ)(\textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{suc}}\, x) & := σ(x) & \textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{wk}}(x) & := \textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{suc}}\, x
\end{align*}
\RW{Instantiation is then defined mutually with composition and lifting:}
\begin{align*}
      (\textcolor{agdablue}{λ.}\, t)  \,[\, σ \,]
                                        & = \textcolor{agdablue}{λ.} (t\,[\, \textcolor{agdablue}{\uparrow}σ \,]) & (σ_1 \
      \textcolor{agdablue}{⨟} \ σ_2)(x) & = σ_1(x)\,[\, σ_2 \,]                                                                                                                              \\
      (t_1 \ t_2)                            \,[\, σ \,]
                                        & = (t_1[\, σ \,]) \ (t_2[\, σ \,])                                       & \textcolor{agdablue}{\uparrow}σ & := \textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{zero}}
      \textcolor{agdablue}{\cdot} (σ \ \textcolor{agdablue}{⨟} \
      \textcolor{agdablue}{\mathsf{wk}})
\end{align*}
\RW{Lifting fixes the bound variable and weakens the rest; composition looks a
variable up in the first map and instantiates the result with the second.}

\section{Rewriting the Sigma Calculus}\label{sec:mis}

The definition of instantiation by mutual recursion with composition
complicates termination. Agda cannot determine that this definition is
structurally recursive (and also lacks mechanisms to justify termination by
well-founded recursion), so we follow Adams~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1} to
resolve the issue by first defining instantiation with renamings. We then
extend the equational theory to handle first-class renamings, and also to
connect renamings with substitutions, with the rules from \textsc{Autosubst 2}.
We use the multi-sorted and scoped version of System~F from \Cref{fig:pre-svm}
as an example for any scoped syntax. The approach described in this section
directly translates to any multi-sorted and scoped syntax as well as simple
intrinsically typed syntax where the type index of the syntax is of type
\AgdaDatatype{Set}.

\subsection{Renaming and Substitution}\label{sec:mis-sub}
\begin{figure*}[t]
      \AgdaNoSpaceAroundCode{}
      \setlength{\mathindent}{0pt}
      \setlength{\AgdaEmptySkip}{\smallskipamount}
      \begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\linewidth}
            \raggedright{}
            \ERen{}
            \ERenOps{}
            \ERenComp{}
      \end{minipage}
      \hfill
      \begin{minipage}[t]{0.50\linewidth}
            \raggedright{}
            \ESub{}
            \ESubEmb{}
            \ESubT{}
            \ESubComp{}
      \end{minipage}
      \caption{The Primitives for Renaming and Substitution}\label{fig:mis-ras}
\end{figure*}

The definitions for renaming and substitution are presented in
\Cref{fig:mis-ras}. The definitions are mostly standard, but we will block the
reduction for most of the renaming and substitution primitives, and describe
their behavior later through rewrite rules.

\RWnote{cut. The old text re-narrated \cref{fig:mis-ras} line by line, which
      costs most of a column and tells the reader what the figure already shows.
      Kept only what the figure cannot say: which definitions are opaque, and why.}

Renamings \AgdaFunction{\_→ᴿ\_} map variables of sort \AgdaBound{s} to variables
of the same sort; substitutions \AgdaFunction{\_→ˢ\_} map them to terms. The
primitives are the expected ones, and most of them are standard, so we comment
only on the choices the figure cannot show.

Every primitive is \AgdaKeyword{opaque}, because a rewrite rule needs a stuck
left-hand side. This includes both lifting operators. Lifting is
\emph{primitive} here rather than defined as
$\bsym{\mathsf{zero}} \bsym{\cdot} (σ \bsym{⨟} \bsym{\mathsf{wk}})$, which is
decision~4 of \cref{fig:decisions} and the reason the applied and lifting rules
do not collapse into $η$. The identity renaming takes its sort argument
explicitly, as Agda frequently fails to infer it. The constants \AgdaFunction{idˢ} and
\AgdaFunction{wkˢ} are not primitive at all but embedded renamings, obtained
through the coercion \AgdaFunction{⟨\_⟩}, and are \AgdaKeyword{INLINE}d so that
they do not reduce on a left-hand side.

Two definitions are shaped by later needs. Lifting a substitution is defined by
instantiation with the weakening \emph{renaming}, which is what makes the
mutual recursion structurally recursive. And instantiation
\AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ᴿ} and \AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ˢ} is defined across both modes,
so that looking a variable up in a map is written \AgdaBound{x}
\AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaBound{σ} \AgdaFunction{]ˢ} rather than as function
application \AgdaBound{σ} \AgdaBound{\_} \AgdaBound{x}. We need this because
the $σ$-calculus rules must be stated using stuck symbols only, with no lambdas
and no function application; \Cref{sec:mis-ex} returns to it.

\subsection{The Sigma Calculus Rules}\label{sec:mis-sig}
\begin{figure*}[t]
      \AgdaNoSpaceAroundCode{}
      \setlength{\mathindent}{0pt}
      \begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
            \raggedright{}
            \EDefLaws{}
            \EInteractLaws{}
      \end{minipage}
      \hfill
      \begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
            \raggedright{}
            \ECoincidenceLaws{}
            \ETraversalLaws{}
            \EMonadLaws{}
      \end{minipage}
      \caption{The Rewrite System~For the Sigma Calculus with First-Class Renamings}\label{fig:mis-sig}
\end{figure*}

\RWnote{\S3.2 described a rule set that no longer exists: it named
      \texttt{⟨⟩-wk-lift}, \texttt{⟨⟩-wk-cons} and \texttt{⟨⟩-lift-lift}, all three
      since retired, called the collapse family a price paid ``under syntactic
      matching'' without saying what generates it, and claimed the interaction
      rules were those of \S2.3. Rewritten around the eight groups, since that is
      what makes 72 a derivation rather than a list.}


\RW{\Cref{fig:mis-sig} shows the substitution-world half of the rule set as Agda
equalities; the renaming-world duplicates and the $\fatsemi$-companions are
omitted for space and are in the supplement. Rather than walk the rules one by
one, we give the groups. The system is a small base closed under four
completion operators, and every rule belongs to exactly one:}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}rlp{0.60\linewidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{\#} & \textbf{group} & \textbf{what it is for} \\
\midrule
16 & traversal    & push an application through one constructor; the only signature-dependent group, two rules per constructor \\
\phantom{0}9 & lookup       & evaluate a constructor-headed map at a variable; these are the definitions of $\bsym{\mathsf{wk}}$, $\bsym{\cdot}$ and $\Uparrow$ \\
16 & map algebra  & normalise a composed map, once per world \\
\phantom{0}7 & fusion & turn two traversals into one, in all four renaming/substitution combinations; the two mixed cases are \textsc{Autosubst}~2's \texttt{compRenSubst} and \texttt{compSubstRen}~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101} \\
\midrule
\phantom{0}7 & continuation & $\mathsf{assoc}$ right-nests $\fatsemi$, so a rule matching a non-variable right operand can no longer see it inside a chain \\
\phantom{0}8 & mode~V & a lookup cannot evaluate an accumulator, so at mode~V the map must be driven back to constructor-headed form \\
\phantom{0}9 & coercion & $\langle\cdot\rangle\text{-}\mathsf{lift}$ and $\langle\cdot\rangle\text{-}\mathsf{comp}$ erase the $\Uparrow$ and $\fatsemi$ heads that the $σ$-rules match on, so every such rule needs a coerced image \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\RW{The four groups above the rule are the content; the three below it are the
price of design decisions, and each comes with a side condition saying when an
image is not needed. Those side conditions are what make the count
predictive rather than empirical. The continuation closure, for instance, demands an image
exactly when the right operand is not a metavariable and the continued
redex has no inner reduction. Four rules match on a non-variable right operand
and have no companion:
$\mathsf{id}_\mathsf{R}$ in both worlds, $\mathsf{interaction}$ and
$\mathsf{lift\text{-}cons}$. In each case the
continued redex escapes by an inner step, which we check as $\mathtt{refl}$
assertions in the supplement. The rule set matches the predicate exactly,
absences included.}

\RW{Three rules are worth naming individually.}

\RW{$\mathsf{coincidence}$, $t\,[\,\langle ξ \rangle\,]^{\mathsf{S}} = t\,[\,ξ\,]^{\mathsf{R}}$,
is the orientation decision: it makes the renaming world the normal form, so
that renaming-only work never leaves it. Everything in the coercion group
follows from having taken that direction.}

\RW{$\mathsf{compositionality}^{\mathsf{RR}}$ exists twice, once folding at mode~T
and once pushing at mode~V. This is the only place where the two modes
disagree on a direction, and they must: at a variable a pending composition can
never be looked up, so it has to be broken apart, whereas at a term
accumulating is exactly what makes the traversal linear.}

\RW{$\langle\cdot\rangle\text{-}\mathsf{comp}$ needs a continuation image by
the continuation closure, and the general one is the exact inverse of
$\langle\cdot\rangle\text{-}\mathsf{split}$, so registering both loops. It is
the one completion image in the whole system that cannot be taken. What
survives is that image restricted to the three prefixes on which the renaming
world can make progress, and those turn out to be exactly the three
renaming-world rules that themselves need continuation images: the same set,
twice.}

\RW{The $η$-laws are proved as lemmas and not registered, for the
left-linearity reason of \cref{sec:pre-sig}. So are
$\mathsf{dist}^{\mathsf{R}}$ and $\mathsf{lift\text{-}cons}^{\mathsf{R}}$,
whose variable instances collide with the lookup rules under \emph{push at
V}, and the two definitions of lifting, which we keep primitive. Ten laws in
total, and they fall into just two causes.}

\RW{All rules are proved before being registered, which is what preserves
consistency. The proofs unfold the opaque definitions and use function
extensionality, conservative over Agda's
theory~\cite{Hofmann1997}; we refer the reader to the supplement.}

